For compressed formats v4l2loopback derives sizeimage from the raw geometry
(width * height * 32 / 8), which is an upper bound on uncompressed pixels
rather than on an encoded frame. At 1920x1080 that reserves 7.91 MiB per
buffer for a stream whose frames are tens to hundreds of kilobytes.

The cost lands twice: v4l2loopback allocates buffer_size * max_buffers, and
v4l2rtspserver raises live555's OutPacketBuffer::maxSize to the device's
buffer size, so every sink and filter allocates that much again. On an
87 MiB board this exhausts memory as soon as an RTSP server attaches.

Honour a caller-supplied sizeimage when it is smaller, so a writer that
knows its own bitrate can bound the allocation. Behaviour is unchanged for
callers that leave sizeimage at zero.

--- a/v4l2loopback.c
+++ b/v4l2loopback.c
@@ -993,7 +993,21 @@
 		if (NULL == format)
 			format = &formats[0];
 
-		pix_format_set_size(&fmt->fmt.pix, format, w, h);
+		{
+			__u32 requested_sizeimage = fmt->fmt.pix.sizeimage;
+
+			pix_format_set_size(&fmt->fmt.pix, format, w, h);
+
+			/* For compressed formats the geometry-derived size is
+			 * an upper bound on raw pixels, not on the encoded
+			 * frame, so it reserves far more than the stream can
+			 * ever use. Honour a smaller caller-supplied bound.
+			 */
+			if ((format->flags & FORMAT_FLAGS_COMPRESSED) &&
+			    requested_sizeimage > 0 &&
+			    requested_sizeimage < fmt->fmt.pix.sizeimage)
+				fmt->fmt.pix.sizeimage = requested_sizeimage;
+		}
 
 		fmt->fmt.pix.pixelformat = format->fourcc;
 		fmt->fmt.pix.colorspace = V4L2_COLORSPACE_SRGB;
